  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1132 AM MDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nA plume of Pacific moisture ahead of a trough swinging through\nthe Great Basin continues to produce scattered showers and \nthunderstorms. Thus far this morning, showers have managed to\nproduce some gusts to around 40 mph. Storm motions remain swift,\nand thus the flash flooding risk remains low. Successive storms\ntraining across sensitive terrain this afternoon and this\nevening could quickly turn attention to flash flooding. Strong\nwinds remain a threat as well, along with small hail, favoring\nnortheast UT and northwest CO.\n\nWith a strong jet streak overhead Saturday night and early\nSunday, showers and storms will likely be able to sustain\nthemselves. High resolution models are preferring to keep this\nthreat confined along the Continental Divide and across\nnortheast UT and northwest CO. Showers and storms will taper\nearly Sunday morning. Convective debris and cloud cover will \nlikely provide some inertia to overcome Sunday as we see another\nround of convection develop ahead of another pinched off trough\nover the Great Basin. Ample moisture awaits, as WPC expanded \ntheir area of 15 percent risk of rainfall exceeding flash flood \nguidance over the entire CWA on Sunday. Storm motions still look\nbrisk on Sunday, but increased training and coverage are likely\nto enhance the flash flood risk, yet again. Wetter boundary \nlayers are expected to suppress some of the gustier outflow \nwinds on Sunday. However, storms should still be capable of \nproducing some 35-45 mph gusts, given the stronger winds aloft.\nThe overhead jet streak doesn't yield much Sunday night and\nmodels are eager to keep some showers going into the early hours\nof Monday, likely tapering around daybreak.\n\nNEXT WEEK:\n\nAs we begin next week, ensembles are supporting PWATs 160-180\npercent of normal through at least Monday, before a slight \ndownturn in moisture for Tuesday. Excessive rain outlooks from \nWPC have highlighted a 25 percent chance for rainfall exceeding \nflash flood guidance for much of eastern UT and western CO on \nMonday. While it's still too early to pinpoint flash flood\ncoverage, flood prone areas across the region are highlighted in\nthis heavy rainfall outlook. Models begin to diverge in \noutcomes by Wednesday leading to rather low forecast confidence \nmidweek and beyond. There still remains a signal for another \npush of monsoonal moisture late next week. This moisture could \nalso be enhanced by a tropical system situated in the eastern \nPacific, which we will have to see evolve to gain a clearer \npicture. As for temperatures, a welcome relief is in store as \ndaytime highs are expected to fall below normal to start next \nweek.",
